It is also possible to load more than one software using `module list`. `module list` shows all loaded
software modules.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module list


Currently Loaded Modulefiles:
 1) R/4.5.0


{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module avail
---------------------------------------------------------------------------- /sc-software/modules_el9/modulefiles ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
abaqus/2025  freesurfer/7.4.1  IMOD/5.1.5   matlab/R2024b       mrtrix3/3.0.5       qupath/0.5.1  relion/4.0.2  samtools/1.17   
fiji/1.54f   fsl/6.0.7.18      julia/1.7.0  matlabruntime/v910  plink/v2.00-250819  R/4.5.0       relion/5.0.0  vscode/4.101.2  

------------------------------------------------------------------------------- /usr/share/Modules/modulefiles -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
dot  module-git  module-info  modules  null  use.own  

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- /usr/share/modulefiles -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
mpi/openmpi-x86_64  

Key:
loaded  modulepath 
```
{: .language-bash}

Let's Load Matlab:

```
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module load matlab/R2024b
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module list
Currently Loaded Modulefiles:
 1) R/4.5.0   2) matlab/R2024b  

```
{: .language-bash}

Load also freesurfer:

```
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module load freesurfer/7.4.1
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module list
Currently Loaded Modulefiles:
 1) R/4.5.0   2) matlab/R2024b   3) freesurfer/7.4.1  
```
{: .language-bash}

So using `module unload` "un-loads" a module, and depending on how a site is
 configured it may also unload all of the dependencies (in our case it does
 not). If we wanted to unload everything at once, we could run `module purge`
 (unloads everything).

```
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module unload freesurfer/7.4.1
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module list
Currently Loaded Modulefiles:
 1) R/4.5.0   2) matlab/R2024b 

{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module purge
{{ site.remote.prompt_node }} module list

No modules loaded
```

{: .language-bash}

Note that `module purge` is informative. It will also let us know if a default
set of "sticky" packages cannot be unloaded (and how to actually unload these
if we truly so desired).
